{"id":"tu-posesivo","es":"tu","en":"your (informal)","sv":"din","pos":"other","track":"words","level":1,"themes":["conectores"],"note":"No accent: it means your. With an accent, tú means you — the accent is the only difference."},
{"id":"su","es":"su","en":"his, her, their","sv":"hans, hennes, deras","pos":"other","track":"words","level":1,"themes":["conectores"],"note":"One Spanish word for his, her, its, their and your. Su tells you nothing about the owner — only the context does. It agrees with the thing owned: su libro, sus libros."},
{"id":"nuestro","es":"nuestro","en":"our","sv":"vår","pos":"other","track":"words","level":1,"themes":["conectores"],"note":"Agrees with the thing owned, not with us: nuestro carro, nuestra casa, nuestros amigos."},
{"id":"que","es":"qué","en":"what","sv":"vad","pos":"other","track":"words","level":1,"themes":["conectores"],"note":"Qué with the accent asks what. Without it, que links a clause (creo que sí) — the accent is the only difference."},
